---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout
  title="resources — tiny soft computer"
  description="Bill of materials, exact pricing, and classroom-friendly alternatives for the tiny soft computer kit."
  active="resources"
>

  <section class="hero" style="padding-top: 2.5rem;">
    <p class="kicker">resources</p>
    <h1>what's in the kit, and what you can swap</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      for educators adapting this kit to their own classroom, budget, or
      what's actually in stock. all prices are from Adafruit's own site,
      pulled at the time this page was last updated, always double-check
      before ordering in bulk since prices and stock shift.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2>bill of materials, per kit</h2>
    <table class="bom">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>item</th>
          <th>unit price</th>
          <th>10–99 qty price</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Adafruit RP2040 Feather ThinkInk for 24-pin E-Paper Displays, STEMMA QT (<a href="https://www.adafruit.com/product/5727">#5727</a>)</td>
          <td>$17.50</td>
          <td>$15.75</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>2.13" 212×104 Flexible Monochrome/4-Gray eInk ePaper Display, ILI0373 (<a href="https://www.adafruit.com/product/4243">#4243</a>)</td>
          <td>$17.50</td>
          <td>$15.75</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Soft Silicone Top 6mm Push-buttons, 20-pack (<a href="https://www.adafruit.com/product/4183">#4183</a>), 2 used per kit</td>
          <td>$2.50 / pack</td>
          <td>$2.25 / pack</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Small Alligator Clip to Male Jumper Wire Bundle, 12-piece (<a href="https://www.adafruit.com/product/3255">#3255</a>), 2 used per kit</td>
          <td>$7.95 / bundle</td>
          <td>$7.16 / bundle</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>USB Type A to Type C Cable, 1m (<a href="https://www.adafruit.com/product/4474">#4474</a>)</td>
          <td>$4.95</td>
          <td>$4.46</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="note">
      the button and clip packs cover several kits each, one 20-pack of
      buttons covers 10 kits, one 12-piece clip bundle covers 6 kits.
      order accordingly rather than one pack per kit.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      <strong>stock warning:</strong> the flexible eInk display (#4243)
      has shown very low stock at times, as few as 5 units. if you're
      ordering a full classroom set, check availability early and order
      well ahead of your workshop date.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2>what you'll also need, not in the kit</h2>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li>a laptop or computer to run Mu Editor</li>
      <li><a href="https://codewith.mu/en/download">Mu Editor</a>, free, works on Mac, Windows, and Linux</li>
      <li>scissors, an xacto knife, or similar for the enclosure</li>
      <li>felt, fabric, cardboard, or other materials for fabrication (see <a href="/fabrication">fabrication</a>)</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2>alternatives</h2>
    <p>
      the flexible eInk display has a real hardware limit: it can only
      redraw once every 180 seconds (see <a href="/about">about</a> for
      why). if that pacing doesn't fit your classroom's timing, or if
      the eInk display is out of stock, a monochrome OLED display swaps
      in cleanly on the exact same microcontroller.
    </p>

    <h3>OLED alternative: what to buy</h3>
    <img
      class="step-image"
      src="/images/oled-326.png"
      alt="Adafruit Monochrome 0.96 inch 128x64 OLED Graphic Display with STEMMA QT connector, product 326."
    >
    <img
      class="step-image"
      src="/images/stemma-qt-cable.png"
      alt="A STEMMA QT / Qwiic 4-pin JST-SH cable, the only wire needed to connect the OLED to the Feather's built-in STEMMA QT port."
    >
    <p class="note" style="margin-top: -0.6rem;">
      the OLED, and the cable that connects it, that's the entire
      wiring step, no soldering, no loose pins.
    </p>
    <table class="bom">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>item</th>
          <th>unit price</th>
          <th>10–99 qty price</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Monochrome 0.96" 128×64 OLED Graphic Display, STEMMA QT (<a href="https://www.adafruit.com/product/326">#326</a>)</td>
          <td>$17.50</td>
          <td>$15.75</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>STEMMA QT / Qwiic JST SH 4-Pin Cable, 50mm (<a href="https://www.adafruit.com/product/4399">#4399</a>)</td>
          <td>$0.95</td>
          <td>$0.95</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      same price as the eInk display, and the same Feather ThinkInk
      board works for both, no need to buy a different microcontroller.
    </p>

    <h3>OLED alternative: wiring</h3>
    <p>
      the Feather ThinkInk has a built-in STEMMA QT port. the OLED also
      has one. connect them with the STEMMA QT cable, that's the entire
      wiring step, no soldering, no loose wires, no pin matching. this is
      actually simpler to wire than the eInk display, which uses the
      separate 24-pin FPC connector instead.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      the button wiring (<code>A2</code> and <code>GND</code>) stays
      exactly the same regardless of which display you use, that part
      of the circuit doesn't change.
    </p>

    <h3>OLED alternative: code changes</h3>
    <p>
      the display driver is different, and since OLED has no refresh
      limit, the code is actually simpler, not just adapted. the
      shuffle-and-wait architecture in the eInk version exists only to
      work around its 180-second limit, none of that is needed here.
    </p>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li>swap the library: use <code>adafruit_displayio_ssd1306</code> instead of <code>adafruit_il0373</code></li>
      <li>swap the display setup: initialize over I2C via <code>board.STEMMA_I2C()</code> instead of the SPI/FourWire setup the eInk display needs, and no busy pin</li>
      <li>no refresh-limit logic at all, the display auto-updates the instant you set <code>display.root_group</code></li>
      <li>each button press can show a new random message immediately, rather than the eInk version's one-press-starts-a-forever-loop approach</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <a class="btn" href="/tiny_soft_computer_oled_code.py" download>download the working OLED code.py</a>
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      this project's earliest prototype was actually built on this exact
      OLED, before switching to eInk for the paper-like quality. the
      file above is a real, tested adaptation of the current
      <code>content</code> list and button logic, not just a sketch of
      what it would need.
    </p>
  </section>

</Layout>